<h2 id="what-to-check">What to Actually Check When Buying an Instrument</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Read the accuracy spec for the range you'll actually use</strong>, not the headline "best case" figure often quoted for a single favourable range.</li>
  <li><strong>Don't equate more displayed digits with a better instrument</strong> — a 4½-digit meter with tight accuracy beats a 6-digit meter with loose accuracy for any measurement where the true value matters, not just the display.</li>
  <li><strong>For acceptance testing, commissioning sign-off, or any measurement with a pass/fail threshold</strong>, accuracy (and its traceability via calibration) is what actually protects you — see our <a href={u('/blog/calibration-interval-guide/')}>calibration interval guide</a> for how that traceability is maintained over time.</li>
</ul>

